DSA Learning — Day 1

📌 Topic: Introduction to Data Structures & Algorithms

Today I started my Data Structures and Algorithms (DSA) learning journey from the fundamentals.

The goal of Day 1 was not to solve difficult problems, but to understand the overall DSA roadmap and how different concepts are connected.

🧠 What I Learned

1. What is DSA?

DSA = Data Structures + Algorithms

- Data Structure: A way of organizing and storing data efficiently.
- Algorithm: A step-by-step procedure used to solve a problem.

2. Why DSA Matters

DSA helps me learn how to:

- Break complex problems into smaller parts
- Choose appropriate data structures
- Design efficient solutions
- Improve problem-solving skills
- Analyze the performance of programs

3. Important DSA Concepts

My learning roadmap includes:

- Arrays
- Strings
- Linked Lists
- Stacks
- Queues
- Hashing
- Recursion
- Searching
- Sorting
- Trees
- Graphs
- Greedy Algorithms
- Backtracking
- Dynamic Programming

4. Complexity Analysis

I was introduced to two important concepts:

Time Complexity
Measures how the running time of an algorithm grows as the input size increases.

Space Complexity
Measures how much additional memory an algorithm requires as the input size increases.

Common complexity patterns I will learn:

O(1)
O(log n)
O(n)
O(n log n)
O(n²)
O(2ⁿ)

5. Problem-Solving Approach

I learned that solving DSA problems is not simply about writing code.

My approach will be:

Understand the problem
        ↓
Identify input and output
        ↓
Break the problem down
        ↓
Choose a suitable data structure
        ↓
Develop an algorithm
        ↓
Implement in Python
        ↓
Test the solution
        ↓
Analyze Time & Space Complexity
        ↓
Improve the solution

🎯 My Learning Goal

I want to develop the ability to think logically and design efficient solutions, rather than simply memorize algorithms.

This repository will document my DSA learning journey, implementations, problem-solving practice, and progress from beginner to advanced concepts.

